      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 595,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on the Earth's surface. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* At standard temperature and pressure, water is a transparent, odorless, and tasteless liquid.\n* However, it is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n\n### 2. The Ecological and Geological View\nWater is the defining feature of our home planet, often called the \"Blue Planet.\"\n* **Global Coverage:** About 71% of the Earth's surface is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **The Water Cycle (Hydrological Cycle):** Water is constantly moving. It evaporates from the oceans and land, rises into the atmosphere to form clouds, falls back down as rain or snow (precipitation), and flows through rivers and groundwater back to the ocean. \n\n### 3. The Biological View (The Essence of Life)\nWater is the medium in which the metabolic processes of life take place. \n* **Human Body:** The human body is made up of about 60% water. The brain is roughly 73% water, the lungs about 83%, and even bones are about 31% water.\n* **Function:** Water acts as a universal solvent. It dissolves nutrients, minerals, and chemicals, transporting them through the bloodstream in humans and the sap in plants. It also helps regulate body temperature and lubricates joints.\n\n### 4. Unique Physical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid.\n* **Ice Floats:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying all aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps coastal areas from experiencing extreme temperature swings.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule of hydrogen and oxygen, but it is also the driving force of nature, weather, and life itself."
